🟠 Finding 3: Internal Network Topology and Diagnostic Disclosure
Severity: Medium
Affected Component: System Tab (Diagnostics), Network Tab, NTP Tab

Description: The web application allows low-privileged users to access raw system diagnostic pages and configuration files. This exposes the internal network routing, active DNS servers, upstream NTP infrastructure, and the underlying operating system architecture.

Observation: While authenticated as the low-privileged Info-User, extensive internal network data was accessible. The interface exposed the internal Linux routing table (revealing internal subnets like 193.150.8.0/21 and gateways like 193.150.15.254), active DNS servers (184.12.21.17, 184.7.12.44), and the raw ntp.conf file which listed upstream corporate time servers (e.g., patek.fr.world.socgen, rolex.fr.world.socgen). Furthermore, the system process list (top) was visible, showing that all backend services (httpd, sshd, ntpd, fastapid) run under the root user, and the physical network configuration exposed MAC addresses and bonding (Link Aggregation) setups.

Impact: This is a significant Information Disclosure vulnerability. By harvesting this data, an attacker gains a precise blueprint of the internal network architecture and OS-level services. Knowing the upstream time servers, internal subnets, and that all processes run as root allows an attacker to plan lateral movement, identify high-value targets, and focus their exploit development on the specific active daemons.

Remediation: Restrict access to all raw system diagnostics (routing tables, process lists, interface configurations, raw .conf files) to Super-User accounts only. Info-Users should only be presented with high-level, aggregated status dashboards.

